## 1. Objective and scope

Develop and execute a Bayesian empirical study to:

- Quantify persistent tensile-strength differences among nominally identical 3D-printed polymer coupons.
- Partition variation among material, printer, build, coupon location, post-processing, conditioning, and testing sources.
- Estimate which measurable factors account for the differences and the magnitude of their effects.
- Discriminate among competing explanations, including:
  1. feedstock variability or moisture;
  2. thermal-history and extrusion variability;
  3. geometric or internal-structure differences;
  4. build-location and environmental effects;
  5. post-processing or storage effects;
  6. tensile-test measurement artifacts.
- Assess whether residual coupon-to-coupon differences remain after identified factors are controlled.

Primary outcome: ultimate tensile strength (UTS). Secondary outcomes: elastic modulus, yield strength where definable, strain at break, failure location, and fracture mode.

The target population is coupons produced using the specified polymer, nominal geometry, slicing settings, printer class, and tensile-test protocol. Generalization beyond those materials, machines, settings, and environmental ranges will be treated as provisional.

## 2. Assumptions

- Coupons can be assigned unique identifiers and tracked from feedstock through fracture.
- Printers, material lots, builds, operators, and test sessions can be randomized or deliberately crossed.
- The tensile frame and environmental and dimensional instruments can be calibrated or checked against traceable references.
- Nominally identical coupons may differ in actual cross-section, internal structure, thermal history, moisture exposure, and alignment.
- Observational associations alone may not identify causes; therefore, the study will combine variance decomposition, controlled interventions, and replication.
- Coupons within the same build or feedstock batch are not statistically independent. Hierarchical models will represent this dependence.
- Missingness, failed prints, premature grip failures, and excluded tests may be informative and will be modeled or reported rather than silently discarded.
- Priors, exclusion criteria, primary contrasts, and stopping rules will be specified before examining tensile outcomes.
- UTS calculated from nominal dimensions and UTS calculated from measured local dimensions answer different questions; both will be retained.
- Measurement error in predictors can attenuate estimated effects and will be modeled where calibration or replicate measurements permit.

## 3. Research design

### 3.1 Staged structure

Use three linked stages.

**Stage A: Measurement-system qualification**

Before comparing manufacturing conditions:

- Verify load, displacement/extensometer, crosshead speed, and dimensional measurement performance.
- Run repeatability and reproducibility studies for width, thickness, mass, moisture measurement, image-derived porosity, and tensile-test setup.
- Test a stable reference material or homogeneous control specimens across test sessions to detect machine or operator drift.
- Estimate grip alignment and bending using strain measured on opposite specimen faces or another suitable alignment check.

**Stage B: Crossed baseline study**

Produce coupons under nominally identical settings while deliberately crossing nuisance factors:

- multiple feedstock lots or spools;
- multiple printers, including repeated use of the same printer;
- multiple builds per printer–material combination;
- multiple positions and orientations within each build;
- multiple operators or setup personnel where relevant;
- randomized post-print conditioning and tensile-test order;
- multiple test days and, if feasible, more than one tensile frame or grip setup.

Use a balanced or near-balanced design. Randomize material-to-printer assignment and coupon positions. Rotate or mirror build layouts across runs so position is not confounded with print order, printer, or material. Scatter coupons from each build across conditioning batches and test sessions.

Include replicated “anchor” conditions throughout the study to quantify temporal drift. Blind tensile-test personnel to manufacturing condition where practical.

**Stage C: Targeted intervention study**

Select interventions from the plausible mechanisms, not solely from baseline correlations. Use a factorial or fractional-factorial design with ranges that are operationally realistic. Candidate manipulated factors include:

- dried versus controlled-moisture feedstock;
- low/nominal/high nozzle temperature;
- verified versus intentionally perturbed extrusion multiplier or flow;
- low/nominal/high cooling or chamber temperature;
- build location;
- raster orientation or path-order controls;
- conditioning time and humidity;
- test alignment or grip protocol, within safe procedural limits.

At minimum, replicate each intervention across printers, builds, and relevant material lots. Include center-point or repeat nominal conditions to identify nonlinear effects and drift. Avoid changing many slicer settings simultaneously unless the contrast is explicitly intended to represent a production recipe.

### 3.2 Experimental units and hierarchy

Define units before data collection:

- feedstock lot/spool;
- printer;
- build;
- coupon;
- coupon section or image region;
- conditioning batch;
- test session;
- tensile frame/operator.

The coupon is the unit for tensile outcomes, but interventions applied to an entire build must be analyzed at the build level. Sample-size planning will use Bayesian simulation based on plausible variance components and minimum practically important effects, accounting for clustering and expected attrition.

### 3.3 Competing explanations and distinguishing observations

Predefine the following predictions:

- **Feedstock/moisture mechanism:** strength tracks measured moisture, lot, drying treatment, voids, or hydrolysis-related indicators across printers. Drying changes outcomes prospectively, and lot effects shrink after moisture and structure are modeled.
- **Thermal/extrusion mechanism:** differences track recorded nozzle/chamber/bed temperatures, extrusion consistency, bead geometry, density, or interlayer bonding. Controlled parameter changes reproduce the strength shift, potentially with nonlinear or interaction effects.
- **Geometry mechanism:** apparent UTS differences diminish when local load-bearing area replaces nominal area. Failure initiates near measured necks, surface defects, or section irregularities.
- **Internal-structure mechanism:** strength tracks void fraction, void morphology, bead contact, or interlayer fusion after external dimensions are controlled. Fracture paths align with those defects.
- **Build-position/environment mechanism:** spatial strength patterns recur at corresponding bed locations, reverse or move when layouts are rotated, and correlate with local thermal or airflow measurements.
- **Post-processing/conditioning mechanism:** randomized conditioning treatments alter strength, and storage history mediates otherwise persistent build or material effects.
- **Testing-artifact mechanism:** differences track test day, operator, frame, grip alignment, bending, strain-rate deviation, or failure near grips. Effects diminish after randomized retesting, alignment correction, or use of independent strain measurement.
- **Latent manufacturing mechanism:** substantial printer/build/coupon residual variance remains despite adequate measurement reliability and controlled testing, indicating unmeasured process heterogeneity rather than known predictors.

## 4. Data or evidence needed

Collect a complete provenance record for every coupon:

- polymer identity, grade, color/additives, supplier, lot, spool, and spool age;
- storage, drying, handling, exposure duration, and measured moisture;
- printer identity, maintenance state, nozzle identity and wear, firmware, slicer version, and machine calibration status;
- complete digital build file, geometry version, toolpath, orientation, raster pattern, layer height, wall count, infill, temperatures, speeds, cooling, flow multiplier, retraction, and print sequence;
- timestamps and machine logs for commanded and measured temperatures, motion, extrusion, pauses, faults, and environmental conditions;
- build-map coordinates and orientation;
- coupon mass and dimensions at standardized locations;
- surface images and roughness indicators;
- internal density, porosity, void morphology, and bead/interlayer structure using a validated nondestructive or sacrificial-sampling method;
- conditioning temperature, humidity, duration, and time from printing to testing;
- tensile frame, load cell, grips, operator, test date, test order, alignment checks, extension measurement method, strain rate, and raw force–extension/strain series;
- failure location, fracture angle, images of fracture surfaces, and classification of grip, gauge-section, or defect-associated failure;
- all print failures, rejected coupons, protocol deviations, missing measurements, and reasons.

Preserve raw sensor streams and images, not only derived summaries. Record calibration data, repeated measurements, and measurement uncertainty for major predictors and outcomes.

## 5. Measurement

### 5.1 Outcomes

Measure and predefine algorithms for:

- maximum tensile load;
- UTS using nominal area;
- UTS using measured minimum external area;
- where feasible, stress based on estimated effective load-bearing area;
- elastic modulus from a prespecified strain interval;
- yield strength or offset yield strength if applicable;
- strain at break;
- toughness from the stress–strain curve;
- failure location and fracture mode.

Use direct strain measurement in the gauge section where feasible. Retain raw curves to diagnose slipping, compliance, noise, and anomalous fracture.

### 5.2 Manufacturing predictors

Measure rather than rely exclusively on machine setpoints:

- actual nozzle, bed, chamber, and ambient temperatures;
- localized environmental gradients across the build area;
- extrusion mass or flow stability;
- finished mass and density;
- width and thickness at several gauge-section locations;
- warpage, surface defects, and roughness;
- moisture immediately before printing and, if relevant, before testing;
- porosity and interlayer/bond metrics;
- print duration, cooling history, pauses, and print-order timing.

Use replicate measurements and blinded image scoring. If human classification is required, assess inter-rater agreement and adjudicate without reference to tensile strength.

### 5.3 Measurement quality

For each instrument and derived feature, document:

- resolution, calibration, uncertainty, and drift;
- repeatability and reproducibility;
- operator dependence;
- detection limits;
- time between sampling and measurement;
- processing algorithms and quality-control thresholds.

Predefine invalid-test rules, such as severe grip slippage or failure outside protocol requirements. Report invalid outcomes separately and model their probability when they may depend on manufacturing condition.

## 6. Analysis

### 6.1 Primary Bayesian model

Fit a hierarchical model to coupon-level outcomes. A representative structure is:

\[
y_i \sim t_\nu(\mu_i,\sigma_i)
\]

\[
\mu_i =
\alpha +
X_i\beta +
Z_i\gamma +
u_{\text{lot}[i]} +
u_{\text{printer}[i]} +
u_{\text{build}[i]} +
u_{\text{position}[i]} +
u_{\text{conditioning}[i]} +
u_{\text{test session}[i]} +
u_{\text{operator}[i]}.
\]

Here, \(X_i\) contains prespecified manufacturing and testing predictors; \(Z_i\) contains selected interactions and nonlinear terms. Build is nested or partially crossed as required by the actual design. Use Student-\(t\) residuals to reduce sensitivity to unexplained extremes, while still investigating them.

Permit heterogeneous residual variance when justified—for example by printer, treatment, or failure mode. Treat repeated outcomes from the same coupon, such as related tensile metrics, with a multivariate model or coordinated secondary models.

### 6.2 Priors

Use weakly informative, scientifically scaled priors for intercepts and main effects; regularizing priors for interactions and nonlinear terms; and half-normal or half-\(t\) priors for standard deviations. Standardize continuous predictors while retaining interpretable effect summaries in original units.

Use prior predictive checks to reject implausible priors before outcome analysis. Conduct sensitivity analyses using broader and more skeptical priors, particularly for proposed causal effects and variance components.

### 6.3 Measurement error and mediation

Where reliability data are available, model latent true moisture, dimensions, temperature, porosity, and other error-prone predictors. Compare:

1. nominal-factor model;
2. model adding measured geometry;
3. model adding material/moisture variables;
4. model adding thermal/extrusion variables;
5. model adding internal-structure variables;
6. model adding testing variables;
7. integrated model.

Use these models to estimate how variance components and treatment effects change as candidate mediators are introduced. Interpret mediation cautiously unless treatment assignment supports causal identification.

### 6.4 Causal contrasts

For randomized interventions, estimate posterior treatment contrasts, interactions, and probabilities that effects exceed a prespecified practical threshold. For observational baseline factors, describe adjusted associations unless exchangeability is defensible.

Use directed causal diagrams prepared in advance to determine adjustment sets and avoid controlling for colliders or indiscriminately adjusting away intervention effects through post-treatment variables.

### 6.5 Model assessment

Perform:

- prior and posterior predictive checks;
- convergence and effective-sample diagnostics;
- residual checks by build, location, time, printer, and failure mode;
- sensitivity to outliers, exclusions, missing-data assumptions, and predictor-error assumptions;
- calibration checks using held-out builds or leave-one-build-out prediction;
- comparison of predictive adequacy using appropriate Bayesian out-of-sample criteria.

Do not choose a mechanism solely by a small difference in predictive score. Require the predicted intervention response, spatial pattern, fracture evidence, or variance reduction specified in advance.

### 6.6 Attribution summaries

Report posterior distributions for:

- each practically relevant factor effect;
- printer, lot, build, position, session, operator, and residual variance components;
- proportion of outcome variance accounted for by each prespecified factor group, with uncertainty;
- expected UTS under standardized conditions;
- probability that each effect exceeds the minimum practically important difference;
- remaining unexplained between-build and between-coupon variation;
- predictive intervals for a future coupon and a future build.

Variance “explained” will be reported as model-dependent and not automatically interpreted as causal.

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

Set a minimum practically important difference in UTS before data collection, along with acceptable predictive uncertainty and maximum tolerable unexplained variance.

Interim analyses may occur only at prespecified completed-build milestones. Continue sampling unless one of the following occurs:

- **Mechanism supported:** the posterior probability that a randomized factor has an effect larger than the practical threshold exceeds 0.95; the observed direction and associated structural or process evidence match the mechanism’s predefined predictions; and the finding replicates across at least two independent builds or relevant printer/material strata.
- **Practical equivalence:** the posterior probability that the factor’s effect lies within the equivalence region exceeds 0.95 across the prespecified operating range.
- **Precision reached:** 95% credible intervals for primary effects and key variance components are narrower than prespecified precision targets, and posterior predictive intervals satisfy the practical requirement.
- **Residual heterogeneity resolved sufficiently:** standardized manufacturing and testing reduce the posterior expected between-build or between-coupon variation below the predefined tolerance.
- **Futility:** posterior predictive simulation indicates a low probability, such as below 0.10, that the maximum feasible additional sample will resolve the primary contrast to the precision target.
- **Feasibility or quality failure:** measurement-system performance, protocol adherence, or attrition makes valid inference unattainable without redesign.

Multiplicity is handled through hierarchical shrinkage and emphasis on prespecified primary contrasts, not by declaring findings from arbitrary posterior thresholds. If multiple mechanisms remain compatible with the evidence, the conclusion will explicitly retain them and identify the next intervention with the highest expected information gain.

## 8. Limitations

- Findings may not generalize to other polymers, colors, additives, printer architectures, geometries, slicer versions, or operating ranges.
- Some internal-structure measurements may be destructive, costly, or available only for sampled coupons, creating missing-data and representativeness concerns.
- Sensor logs may not capture the local bead temperature, pressure, molecular orientation, crystallinity, or interdiffusion that directly governs bonding.
- Several mechanisms may produce similar observable signatures and may interact, limiting unique attribution.
- Build-level treatment assignment limits effective sample size even when many coupons are printed per build.
- Deliberate parameter perturbations may not reproduce naturally occurring faults.
- Conditioning and polymer aging can continue during the study, requiring strict timing and environmental control.
- Effective load-bearing area and fracture-mode classifications may carry substantial measurement uncertainty.
- Bayesian conclusions depend on model structure and priors; sensitivity analyses can expose but not eliminate this dependence.
- A reduction in residual variance after adjustment does not by itself establish causality. Strong causal conclusions require randomized manipulation, temporal ordering, replication, and mechanism-consistent evidence.

